    <article class="py-12">
        <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
            <div class="prose prose-lg text-gray-700">

                <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-8">A firearm that went missing in January 1994 was handed to the owner's son recently. Somebody had filed a report, and it was still on the books.</p>

                <p>According to <a href="https://wcti12.com/news/local/stolen-sampson-county-firearm-returned-to-family-32-years-later" class="text-av-gold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WCTI 12</a>, Sampson County Sheriff Jimmy Thornton said several firearms were taken in a breaking and entering in the Ingold community in January 1994. One was a Savage .20-gauge/.22 Magnum over-and-under belonging to Morris Ezzell. It had been his father's, and the sheriff's office said it carried deep sentimental value for the family.</p>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">What the sheriff's office said happened</h2>
                <p>Per WCTI's September 24 report:</p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                    <li>Investigators said the gun stayed listed in the National Crime Information Center (NCIC) database as stolen for 32 years.</li>
                    <li>On August 25, investigators got a notification from Maryland about a firearm matching the stolen gun's description.</li>
                    <li>The gun had been found in the closet of a recently purchased home and taken to a Maryland pawn shop for consignment.</li>
                    <li>Investigators confirmed it was the firearm reported stolen in 1994, and the pawn shop shipped it back to Sampson County.</li>
                    <li>Morris Ezzell asked that it go to his son, Marshall, as a surprise. Investigator Jerry Murphy made the presentation. Deputy Ed Vann, who took the original report in 1994, was there.</li>
                </ul>

                <p>The story doesn't say how the match was made, what the serial number was, or how the gun ended up in that closet. We won't guess. What it does show is simple: the record was still there when the call came.</p>

                <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-6 my-8">
                    <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                        <i class="bi bi-lightbulb text-amber-600 text-xl mt-1"></i>
                        <div>
                            <h4 class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-0">A theft report can outlast everyone's memory. Your own copy of the details is what lets you say "that one's mine."</h4>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">Why a 32-year-old report still mattered</h2>
                <p>According to the FBI's NCIC Gun File Quick Reference Card (July 2024), as posted by the NC State Bureau of Investigation, stolen, lost, and felony gun records do not expire. They are retained until the record is cleared, canceled, or located. In Sampson County, that meant a 1994 report was still on file in 2026 when a firearm matching the description turned up.</p>

                <p>That's the agency side. Your side is what you handed them, and what you can still produce.</p>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">The heirloom problem</h2>
                <p>Look at what this gun was: a family piece, passed down from a father. Those are the guns least likely to have a receipt, a box, or a photo. If you own one, ask yourself what you could give an officer today:</p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                    <li>Make, model, and gauge or caliber</li>
                    <li>The serial number, exactly as stamped (some older guns have it in more than one place, so check)</li>
                    <li>Photos, overall and a serial close-up</li>
                    <li>Anything that makes it yours: engraving, repairs, a cracked stock, who it came from</li>
                    <li>A note on where it came from and who it went to, if it's an inherited piece</li>
                </ul>

                <p>Older guns tend to get missed when people build a list. They aren't in the safe's "recent purchases" pile, and nobody remembers to write them down.</p>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">What to keep together</h2>
                <p>For each firearm, keep these in one private place you can open from another device:</p>

                <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl p-6 my-8">
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-4"><i class="bi bi-check2-square text-av-gold mr-2"></i>Have this ready before you need it</h4>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Make / manufacturer</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Model and caliber or gauge</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Serial number, exactly as stamped</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Photos, both overall and a serial close-up</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Purchase, transfer, or inheritance notes</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Marks, repairs, and other details that identify that specific gun</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Insurance details, if the collection is scheduled</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <p>If something goes missing, report it promptly and give the full serial. Police need the number to enter the theft, and insurers usually ask for the police report and proof of ownership and value. Check your local reporting rules. This is not legal advice. For the after-theft steps, see <a href="/blog/stolen-firearm-checklist.html" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">What to Do When a Firearm Is Stolen</a>. If a gun is recovered later, matching it back is serial-number work: <a href="/blog/when-stolen-guns-come-back.html" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">When Stolen Guns Come Back, Matching Them to You Takes Serials</a>. For inherited guns, see <a href="/blog/estate-planning-firearms.html" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">Estate Planning for Firearms</a>.</p>

                <p>Printable version: <a href="https://arsenalvault.com/claim-prep-checklist.html?utm_source=blog&utm_medium=cta&utm_campaign=sampson_return&utm_content=checklist_mid" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">Get the free claim-prep checklist</a>.</p>

                <p>Keep the record private. A serial list doesn't belong in a group chat, a forum post, or the background of a photo on social media.</p>

